# Example: PORTS=8080,443,8081 # Uncomment and modify to use with your application. Leave empty or comment out if not needed. PORTS is expected to be a comma-separated list of valid port numbers (1-65535). If no PORTS are specified, the application will terminate with an error message. The code will attempt to parse the PORTS string into individual port numbers, validate each one, sort them, and then print each port number. If any errors occur during this process, such as invalid port numbers or issues with parsing the input, the application will catch the exception and print an error message before terminating. This allows you to configure which ports your application should listen on by setting the PORTS environment variable accordingly. For example, to run the application on ports 8080, 443, and 8081, you would set PORTS=8080,443,8081 in your .env file. If you do not need to specify any ports, simply leave the PORTS line commented out or remove it entirely from your .env file. In that case, the application will output "No PORTS environment variable provided" and exit with an error status. This ensures that your application can handle both cases where specific ports are configured and when they are not. By using the PORTS environment variable, you gain flexibility in managing how your application binds to network ports without hardcoding these values directly in your source code. This approach promotes better security and easier maintenance of your application's configuration. Remember to keep sensitive information like port numbers secure and avoid exposing them unnecessarily in your codebase or version control systems. Always ensure that your .env files are properly secured and not included in public repositories unless absolutely necessary. # END OF EXAMPLE # Uncomment and modify as needed for your specific use case. Leave empty or comment out if no custom port configuration is required. # PORTS= # Example: PORTS=8080,443,8081 # Uncomment and modify to use with your application. Leave empty or comment out if not needed. PORTS is expected to be a comma-separated list of valid port numbers (1-65535). If no PORTS are specified, the application will terminate with an error message. The code will attempt to parse the PORTS string into individual port numbers, validate each one, sort them, and then print each port number. If
